Below is a **self‑contained C# console program** that shows the most common ways to work with records in modern C# (C# 9 +).  
It demonstrates

1. **Defining a positional record**  
2. **Creating an instance**  
3. **Deconstructing**  
4. **`with‑expression` (copy + modifications)**  
5. **Value‑based equality**  
6. **Inheritance of records**  
7. **A record struct**  

```csharp
// <snippet_RecordDemo>
using System;

namespace RecordDemo
{
    // 1️⃣ Positional record – automatically gets readonly properties, ctor, deconstructors, Equals/HashCode, ToString.
    public record Person(string FirstName, string LastName, int Age);

    // 2️⃣ Inheritance – a derived record can add new properties.
    public record Employee(Person PersonInfo, string Department)
        : Person(PersonInfo.FirstName, PersonInfo.LastName, PersonInfo.Age)
    {
        // You can also add methods or new members if you wish.
        public string FullName => $"{FirstName} {LastName}";
    }

    // 3️⃣ Record struct – value type record (behaves like a struct but with record semantics).
    public readonly record struct Point(int X, int Y);

    class Program
    {
        static void Main()
        {
            // ── Create an instance of the base record
            var alice = new Person("Alice", "Smith", 28);
            Console.WriteLine($"Original: {alice}");

            // ── Deconstruct into local variables
            var (first, last, age) = alice;
            Console.WriteLine($"Deconstructed -> First: {first}, Last: {last}, Age: {age}");

            // ── Copy with modification (immutability + easy updates)
            var olderAlice = alice with { Age = 29 };
            Console.WriteLine($"Modified: {olderAlice}");

            // ── Equality is value‑based
            var anotherAlice = new Person("Alice", "Smith", 28);
            Console.WriteLine($"alice == anotherAlice : {alice == anotherAlice}"); // True
            Console.WriteLine($"alice == olderAlice   : {alice == olderAlice}");   // False

            // ── Record inheritance example
            var manager = new Employee(alice, "Engineering");
            Console.WriteLine($"Employee: {manager}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Full Name: {manager.FullName}");

            // ── Record struct usage
            var p1 = new Point(10, 20);
            var p2 = p1 with { Y = 30 };
            Console.WriteLine($"Point1: {p1}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Point2: {p2}");
        }
    }
}
// </snippet_RecordDemo>
```

### What the code shows

| Feature | What you see | Why it matters |
|---------|--------------|----------------|
| **Positional record** (`Person`) | `Person` auto‑generates read‑only properties, a constructor, deconstructors, `Equals`, `GetHashCode`, and `ToString`. | Simplifies data‑carrier types. |
| **Deconstruction** (`var (first, last, age) = alice;`) | Pulls values into locals. | Works with pattern matching. |
| **`with` expression** (`alice with { Age = 29 };`) | Creates an immutable copy with one property changed. | Immutable updates are concise. |
| **Value‑based equality** (`alice == anotherAlice`) | Records compare by value, not by reference. | Makes identity comparison intuitive. |
| **Inheritance** (`Employee : Person`) | Derived record can add fields while still keeping base semantics. | Enables extending data models. |
| **Record struct** (`Point`) | A value‑type record (like a struct) with record semantics. | For small, copy‑by‑value types. |

Run the program in a .NET 6/7 environment (or newer) and you'll see output like:

```
Original: Person { FirstName = Alice, LastName = Smith, Age = 28 }
Deconstructed -> First: Alice, Last: Smith, Age: 28
Modified: Person { FirstName = Alice, LastName = Smith, Age = 29 }
alice == anotherAlice : True
alice == olderAlice   : False
Employee: Employee { PersonInfo = Person { FirstName = Alice, LastName = Smith, Age = 28 }, Department = Engineering }
Full Name: Alice Smith
Point1: Point { X = 10, Y = 20 }
Point2: Point { X = 10, Y = 30 }
```

Feel free to tweak the code to suit your own data models – records give you a lot of power with very little boilerplate.
